FPTP:- 

An electoral system where the person with the most number of votes is elected. Victory is achieved by having one more vote than other contenders - it is also called a plurality system.

Strengths:-
- Stops extremists (?)
- Simple and easy to understand (Turnout)
- Strong single party government
- Close MP + Constituent link

Weaknesses:-
- Two party system
- Minority rule
- Wasted votes (Safe Seats + Loses)
- Tactical voting
- Spoiler effect
- Bad for smaller parties

*****************************************************************************************************

SUPPLEMENTARY VOTE:-

This is a majoritarian system. The voter makes two choices (hence the term ‘supplementary’). If one candidate obtains over 50% on the first vote then the contest is complete. If no candidate attains this level, all but the top two candidates are eliminated. Then the supplementary choices are re-distributed and whoever gets most votes from the remaining two, wins the seat. This form of voting for mayors and police and crime commissioners was ended by the Elections Act in 2022.

*****************************************************************************************************

ATV:-

- FPTP causes [[tactical voting]]
- The least popular candidate is eliminated until a candidate gets a majority
    - The votes of these candidates then go to their next ranked candidate
- No [[spoiler effect]]
- In SV there is few wasted votes

Strengths:-
- Majority result - SV ensures that the winning candidate has a clear majority of the votes increasing their legitimacy. This should lead to a strong and single party government. - 2021 London mayoral election Sadiq Khan won 55% of the vote - e.g. Boris Johnson in 2017, with the weakest majority but still a majority
- Voters choice - Voters have more choice than in FPTP, knowing that they can vote for a smaller without wasting their vote. e.g. Sadiq Khan got 192,313 transfers in 2021
- Simple system - unlike [[proportional]] systems it is relatively simple e.g. the system can easily understand how ranking a first and second choice candidate - good for [[Turnout]]
- Extremist parties - requiring the winner to gain a simple majority means it is unlikely that extremist parties getting in e.g. Reclaim failing miserably in 2021

Weaknesses:-
- Two-party dominance - the elimination of all but two means third parties are unlikely to do well e.g. never been a non Conservative or Labour mayor
- A false majority - a candidate needs only to gain a majority of the ‘valid vote’, meaning the votes that count e.g. 276863 transfer votes in London 2021
- Wasted votes - large number of wasted votes e.g. nearly a million wasted first votes in London 2021
- [[Tactical voting]] - may encourage tactical second voting e.g. large number of transfer votes for Labour + Conservative

*****************************************************************************************************

SINGLE TRANSFERABLE VOTE:-

It’s a proportional system using large multi-member constituencies. Voters rank as many candidates as they want to. When votes are counted, the Droop formula is used to determine the quota of votes a candidate needs in order to be elected. Any candidate who reaches this quota is then elected. Any surplus votes for that candidate are then redistributed on the basis of voters’ second preferences. If no other candidates are elected, the biggest losers are eliminated and their votes are redistributed based on second preferences. This continues until all seats in the multi-member constituency are filled.

- [[Proportional]] means percentage of votes = percentage of MP’s e.g. 30% of votes = 30% of seats
- Constituencies send more than one representative
- Droop formulae → Votes/N of Reps = Quota to win
    - e.g. 100/3 = 33.3% to win
- Biggest loser is knocked out if number of reps isn’t filled and biggest losers votes go to second representatives
- Can have more than one candidate from party at a time
- If a candidate has more votes than the quota the extra is given to their second choices

Strengths -
- [[Proportional]] result - STV is the most [[proportional]] of the vote cast e.g.
- Voter choice - Voters have a great degree of choice, not only between parties but also within parties e.g.
- Greater representation - Electing someone who is closer to you ideologically e.g.

Weaknesses -
- More complicated - maths e.g.
- An unlikely single-party government - weak [[Coalition Government]]s e.g.
- Constituency link - weaker local links e.g.








